denmark is not a very big place the  danish realm  is absolutely massive and this is  exclusively down to the inclusion of  greenland  and given its size its important  position and its distance from the  nation of denmark this raises a question  how did it come to be in the possession  of the danes and how did they keep it  so by the 10th century the danes and  norse vikings had been sailing all over  europe doing what they do best  and part of that were settling lands  like the pharaoh islands in iceland  some norsemen opted to go west and came  across greenland where they too  settled and it wasn't long until one of  these settlers late for eriksen  travelled from greenland to north  america becoming the first european to  ever do so  greenland was already poorly populated  and as such was mostly ignored by the  norwegian monarchy although it was a  formal part of the kingdom  and this was mostly down to a lack of  communication since a round trip from  greenland to trondheim took many months  by the 15th century mostly due to the  plague the norwegians were gone and in  their absence the portuguese laid claim  to the land  that said they never did anything there  and so everyone just ignored this claim  at the turn of the 16th century empire  was very fashionable and not wanting to  be left out the kingdom of denmark  norway ruled by one man with two  different titles sought to colonize  greenland again  and in the 18th century greenland joined  iceland and the pharaoh islands as a  formal part of the kingdom of norway  seems pretty simple who it would belong  to but in came napoleon to complicate  things  as always now denmark made one silly  decision during napoleon's tenure which  was to be his ally  this led to two outcomes first was that  britain shelled copenhagen  finally teaching the danes how it feels  to have a bunch of angry ships turn up  off your shore and set things on fire  the second outcome was that the kingdom  of denmark norway lost norway to 